course: dictionary
lesson: 9729
title: Reading a plant word in context
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A sunflower is a very tall plant with a large flower. Its flower has a brown centre and yellow petals, and its seeds can be eaten or pressed into oil.
explain: Context helps you picture a word. «Young sunflowers turn from east to west with the sun during the day.» The words turn and with the sun tell you the young plant moves its head to follow the sun.
explain: «Sunflowers live for one summer: they grow, flower, make seeds and die.» Here live for one summer means their whole life lasts one summer. Read the words around a new word and ask what would make sense.
fact: «Sunflower» means a very tall plant with a large flower, a brown centre and yellow petals.
fact: Sunflower seeds are food for people and birds.
fact: Sunflowers came from North America and reached Europe in the 1500s.
fact: Van Gogh painted a series of sunflower pictures in 1888 and 1889 to decorate a room for his friend, the painter Paul Gauguin.
exercise: What colour is the centre of a sunflower? | Green | Yellow | Blue | *Brown
exercise: «Sunflowers live for one summer.» How long does a sunflower live? | *One summer | For a hundred years | Every winter | Ten years
exercise: What can the seeds of a sunflower be used for? | Making paper | Building houses | Making glass | *Eating, or making into oil
test: «Young sunflowers turn to follow the sun.» What does turn mean here? | Stay completely still | Fade and die | Fall off the stem | *Move round
test: Where did sunflowers first come from? | Australia | *North America | Africa | Asia
exam: «Grown flower heads stay facing east, which warms them in the morning and brings more bees.» Why do grown sunflowers face east? | *To warm themselves in the morning and bring more bees | To catch the wind | To grow taller at night | To hide from the sun
